15 Feb 2026
Consider that we treat all the natural numbers as prime
candidates, unless we remove them in the style of Eratosthenes
sieve
Starting with 2 as prime, we remove all larger multiples of 2,
then the set of prime candidates includes the number 2 and all
odd numbers.
For any prime candidate we choose, then this leads to an infinite
Cunningham chain of prime candidates (of either type).
If we sieve out multiples of 3, then we have the numbers 2, 3 and
a cycle of numbers which are equivalent to 1 or 5 modulo 6
For Cunningham chains of type 1, then for chains starting with a
value a_1 equivalent to 1 (mod 6), then a_2 is equivalent to 3
(mod 6) and so is known to be composite.  So the values
equivalent to 1 (mod 6) end a CC of type 1.
For any value equivalent to 5 (mod 6), then the next item in the
chain is also equivalent to 5 (mod 6) and so this leads to
infinite Cunningham chains of type 1 in the set of prime
candidates which have multiples of 2 and 3 removed.
Similarly for CC_2, a_1 equiv to 5 (mod 6) leads to a_2 equiv to
3 (mod 6), so ends the chain.  But for a_i equiv to 1 (mod 6),
then next value in the chain is equiv 1 (mod 6), so infinite CC
of type 2.

Let's proceed to sieving multiples of 5 from our prime candidate
set.  This leaves, 2, 3 and 5 and values which are equivalent to
1,7,11,13,17,19,23,29 (mod 30).
For CC_1, we can see that if a chain starts with a value
equivalent to 1,7,13,19 (mod 30), these are all equiv 1 (mod 6),
so as above end a chain.
Consider a chain, a_1 equiv 11 (mod 30), then a_2 is
equiv 23 (mod 30), and a_3 is equiv 17 (mod 30), then a_4 is
equiv 5 (mod 30) and so ends the chain.  All chains which have
values which are equiv 11, 17 or 23 (mod 30), then are finite.
For values equiv 29 (mod 30), then the next value is also equiv
29 (mod 30), leading to candidate infinite chains of type 1.

Similar arguments for CC_2, lead to finite chains for all values
of prime candidates, except for those equiv 1 (mod 30).

If we consider the sieving of multiples of 7, and want to
consider possible long chains, then we need to consider the
values:
29,59,89,149,179,209 (mod 210) (noting that the value 119 (mod
210) has been removed since 119 is a multiple of 7 (119 = 7.17).
Consider

a_1 = 29, a_2 = 59 and a_3 = 119 (mod 210)

then chains
of type 1 which start from value which are equivalent to 29 or 59
(mod 210), are finite.
Consider

a_1 = 89, a_2 = 179, a_3 = 149, a_4 = 89, .. (mod 210)
a_1 = 209, a_2 = 209, ..

then chains which have values which are equiv to 89, 149, 179 or
209 (mod 210) are infinite candidate chains.

(I'm going to stop including type 2 discussion now)

If we sieve multiples of 11, then consider the type 1 infinite
candidate chains.  If we consider the candidate values in the
form 30k-1, then for the values modulo 210 the candidate
cycle of k = (3,6,5) mod 7 can be "expanded" into two cycles which
are modulo 77.

k = (3,6,12,24,48,19,38,76,75,73,69,61,45,13,26,52,27,54,31,62,47,17,34,68,59,41,5,10,20,40) mod 77
k = (33,66,55) mod 77

Also the value 209 (mod 210) which equating to 30k-1 where k=7
can be similarly expanded to

k = (7,14,28,56,35,70,63,49,21,42) mod 77
k = 77 or 0 (mod 77)

If we consider the CC_1 values a_i and assume that a_1 = 1 (mod
11), then the CC_1 values, then the remainders modulo 11 cycle as
follows:

(1,3,7,4,9,8,6,2,5,0) mod 11

This implies that for CC_1 values which have remainders in the
above cycle, then the cycle contains a value divisible by 11,
hence this means finite type 1 chains.
This remainder cycle maps onto the k=(3,..) cycle and the
k=(7,..) cycle.  The values of k which correspond to values
divisible by 11 are k=7,73,62,40 (mod 77)
Any chain which has a value (30k-1) where k is in either of these
cycles will lead to finite type 1 chains (of length 9 at most).

For the values which have k=(33,66,55) or k=0 mod 77, it is
observed that the all the values (30k-1) for these values of k
are not divisible by 11 but are equivalent to 10 modulo 11.

This implies that any type 1 chains which are equivalent to
989,1649,1979 or 2309 (mod 2310) are infinite candidate chains.

